          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Nineveh commonly symbolizes a wicked Gentile city or society that, in Jonah and the Gospels, repents and receives divine mercy, while also functioning in Nahum and Zephaniah as an oppressive imperial power judged by God.\",\"book_summary\":\"Nineveh signifies repentant Gentiles who will rise in judgment against those who reject Jesus, with the added description of it as a great city of the sea.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A wicked Gentile people or city whose repentance exemplifies divine mercy and condemns unrepentant hearers.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Repentant Gentile people who condemn those rejecting Jesus at the judgment.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book selects the Matthean repentant-Gentile application and does not include Nineveh’s other standard function as the violent Assyrian imperial city under divine judgment. It also specifically limits the contrasted unrepentant group to those rejecting Jesus.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book’s identification is explicitly represented within the consensus, but it covers only one major strand of Nineveh’s broader canonical symbolism.\",\"rationale\":\"The central identification—repentant Gentiles serving as witnesses against unrepentant hearers—is shared almost exactly by the book and consensus. The relationship is refined rather than a full match because the book makes this one Gospel application the identification of Nineveh, excluding or leaving aside the established judgment-on-empire cases found in Nahum and Zephaniah.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Matthew 12:41 strongly supports repentance and rising in judgment against Jesus’ generation, but it does not by itself establish the added phrase “great city of the sea,” and it only implicitly supports the generalized symbolic formulation “Gentile people who reject Jesus.”\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
